		),
	]
}

// Tools
func tools(): Slide[5] {
	return [
		Slide(
			title: "What an error looks like",
			titlePt: "Como um erro aparece",
			download: false,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "Every diagnostic starts with where it happened, `file:line: message`, the shape editors expect, so hooking one up takes a compile command and a pattern. A compile error has nothing in front of that. A usage error, like `hive build` with no entrypoint, starts with `hive:`, because it is about what was typed rather than about anybody's source. Both go to standard error and exit with 1, so `hive emit main.hive > main.go` never writes an error into the Go. A failing test is an answer, not a malfunction, so its report goes to standard output.\n\nEvery message is held to the same standard:\n\n- it names the thing: `parts` wants `Str[3]` and this is `Str[dyn]`, never \"type mismatch\";\n- it says what to do: a bounds error says how to guard the index, and a `sort` that cannot sort in place names both fixes;\n- it says why, when the why is the point: `hive.math.max(0, health)` is refused because Hive never widens a number behind your back;\n- it does not guess: a miscased keyword is reported as a keyword written the wrong way, and as a name the language has taken.\n\nEach pass reports everything it finds, but the passes run one after another and the first to find anything stops the rest. A program with a type error and an unguarded index hears about the type error first, and about the index once it gets that far. The lexer, the parser and the loader point at the exact line, and an import naming no file is reported on the line of its `import`. Later passes see one flat program, so what they find lands on a declaration: its line and ``in `main`:``, in the file the declaration came from, named as that file spells it.",
			explanationPt: "Todo diagnóstico começa por onde aconteceu, `file:line: message`, o formato que os editores esperam, então integrar um leva um comando de compilação e um padrão. Um erro de compilação não tem nada antes disso. Um erro de uso, como `hive build` sem ponto de entrada, começa com `hive:`, porque é sobre o que foi digitado, e não sobre o código de alguém. Os dois vão para a saída de erro e terminam com 1, então `hive emit main.hive > main.go` nunca escreve um erro dentro do Go. Um teste que falha é uma resposta, não um defeito, então o relatório dele vai para a saída padrão.\n\nToda mensagem segue o mesmo padrão:\n\n- diz a coisa pelo nome: `parts` quer `Str[3]` e isto é `Str[dyn]`, nunca \"tipos incompatíveis\";\n- diz o que fazer: um erro de limites diz como proteger o índice, e um `sort` que não consegue ordenar no lugar cita as duas correções;\n- diz por quê, quando o porquê é o ponto: `hive.math.max(0, health)` é recusado porque o Hive nunca alarga um número pelas suas costas;\n- não chuta: uma palavra-chave com maiúscula errada é informada como uma palavra-chave escrita do jeito errado, e como um nome que a linguagem já tomou.\n\nCada passo informa tudo o que encontra, mas os passos rodam um depois do outro, e o primeiro a encontrar algo interrompe os demais. Um programa com um erro de tipo e um índice sem proteção fica sabendo primeiro do erro de tipo, e do índice quando chegar até lá. O lexer, o parser e o carregador apontam a linha exata, e um import que não nomeia arquivo nenhum é informado na linha do `import`. Os passos seguintes veem um único programa plano, então o que eles encontram cai numa declaração: a linha dela e ``in `main`:``, no arquivo de onde a declaração veio, com o nome escrito como aquele arquivo o escreve.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "Three mistakes, and three messages", captionPt: "Três erros, e três mensagens", code: `proc main(): void \{
	Str[3] parts = split("north,south", ",")
	echo parts[0]

	health := 3
	echo hive.math.max(0, health)
}`, codePt: `proc main(): void \{
	Str[3] parts = split("north,south", ",")
	echo parts[0]

	health := 3
	echo hive.math.max(0, health)
}`),
				Example(caption: "What hive check says about it", captionPt: "O que o hive check diz sobre isso", code: "$ hive check colony.hive\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the declaration of `parts` wants Str[3] and this is Str[dyn]\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the first argument of `hive.math.max` wants Float and this is Int\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the second argument of `hive.math.max` wants Float and this is Int\n\n# All three land on line 1, the line `main` is declared on: after the\n# loader, a position is a declaration's.", codePt: "$ hive check colony.hive\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the declaration of `parts` wants Str[3] and this is Str[dyn]\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the first argument of `hive.math.max` wants Float and this is Int\ncolony.hive:1: in `main`: the second argument of `hive.math.max` wants Float and this is Int\n\n# As três caem na linha 1, a linha em que `main` é declarado: depois do\n# carregador, uma posição é a de uma declaração."),
				Example(caption: "A message that says what to do", captionPt: "Uma mensagem que diz o que fazer", code: "$ hive check guard.hive\nguard.hive:1: in `main`: cannot prove this index is in range: it is a computed expression, and there is nothing to hang a guard on. Bind it to a name first, then guard that\n\n$ hive check shouted.hive\nshouted.hive:5: `Proc` is a keyword written the wrong way: every keyword in Hive is lower case, and only lower case. Write `proc` — or, if a name was meant, one the language has not already taken. A keyword is reserved however it is spelled, so `Proc` is no more a name than `proc` is.", codePt: "$ hive check guard.hive\nguard.hive:1: in `main`: cannot prove this index is in range: it is a computed expression, and there is nothing to hang a guard on. Bind it to a name first, then guard that\n\n$ hive check shouted.hive\nshouted.hive:5: `Proc` is a keyword written the wrong way: every keyword in Hive is lower case, and only lower case. Write `proc` — or, if a name was meant, one the language has not already taken. A keyword is reserved however it is spelled, so `Proc` is no more a name than `proc` is."),
				Example(caption: "Where an error in a module lands", captionPt: "Onde cai um erro num módulo", code: "$ hive check main.hive\nmain.hive:2: cannot read `lib/missing.hive`: open lib/missing.hive: no such file or directory\n\n# With the import fixed, the mistake inside the module is reported there,\n# by the name the module gives it.\n$ hive check main.hive\nlib/text.hive:1: in `shout`: `+` wants two of one type, and these are Str and Int\n\n$ hive build\nhive: `hive build` needs an entrypoint: `hive build <entrypoint.hive>`.", codePt: "$ hive check main.hive\nmain.hive:2: cannot read `lib/missing.hive`: open lib/missing.hive: no such file or directory\n\n# Com o import corrigido, o erro dentro do módulo é informado lá,\n# pelo nome que o módulo dá a ele.\n$ hive check main.hive\nlib/text.hive:1: in `shout`: `+` wants two of one type, and these are Str and Int\n\n$ hive build\nhive: `hive build` needs an entrypoint: `hive build <entrypoint.hive>`."),
				Example(caption: "Wiring up an editor", captionPt: "Integrando um editor", code: `" In Vim or Neovim. hive check runs every compiler pass and stops before
" the Go toolchain, so it answers as fast as the front end does and writes
" nothing next to the file — which is what makes it usable on every save.
setlocal makeprg=hive\ check\ %:S
setlocal errorformat=%f:%l:\ %m,%-G%.%#

" :make fills the quickfix list, and :cn walks the errors. The %-G item
" drops every line that is not a diagnostic.`, codePt: `" No Vim ou no Neovim. hive check roda todos os passos do compilador e para
" antes do Go, então responde tão rápido quanto o front end e não escreve
" nada ao lado do arquivo — o que o torna usável a cada vez que se salva.
setlocal makeprg=hive\ check\ %:S
setlocal errorformat=%f:%l:\ %m,%-G%.%#

" :make preenche a lista quickfix, e :cn percorre os erros. O item %-G
" descarta toda linha que não é um diagnóstico.`),
			],
		),
		Slide(
			title: "Building for another platform",
			titlePt: "Construindo para outra plataforma",
			download: false,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "A build ends with the Go toolchain, so a Hive program is built for another platform the way any Go program is: by telling the toolchain which one. `hive build` takes a `--target`, a `goos/goarch` pair, and checks the pair before compiling anything. `go tool dist list` names every platform the toolchain knows; a pair it does not name is refused immediately, as a mistake in what was typed, rather than ten seconds later by a build failing in a language you did not write. The list is asked for, not kept, so a platform Go adds later needs no change in Hive.\n\n- **The output's name says what it is for.** A host build of `main.hive` is `main`, and a cross build is `main-linux-arm64`, so a cross build never quietly replaces the local one. The `.exe` follows the target, not the machine: `--target windows/amd64` writes `main-windows-amd64.exe` from any machine.\n- **No C compiler is needed.** The runtime is all Go, so a cross build runs with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, and a build for this machine is given no environment at all: byte for byte the same build as without `--target`.\n- **Only `build` and `export` take the flag**, the two commands that write something for a platform. `check`, `emit`, `test` and `container` refuse it rather than quietly ignoring it. With `hive run`, everything after the entrypoint belongs to the program, so a `--target` written there is passed straight to it.",
			explanationPt: "Um build termina na toolchain do Go, então um programa Hive é construído para outra plataforma do jeito que qualquer programa Go é: dizendo à toolchain qual. `hive build` recebe um `--target`, um par `goos/goarch`, e verifica o par antes de compilar qualquer coisa. `go tool dist list` nomeia toda plataforma que a toolchain conhece; um par que ele não nomeia é recusado na hora, como um erro no que foi digitado, e não dez segundos depois, por um build falhando numa linguagem que você não escreveu. A lista é consultada, não guardada, então uma plataforma que o Go ganhar depois não exige mudança nenhuma no Hive.\n\n- **O nome do que sai diz para que serve.** Um build local de `main.hive` é `main`, e um build cruzado é `main-linux-arm64`, então um build cruzado nunca substitui o local em silêncio. O `.exe` segue o target, não a máquina: `--target windows/amd64` escreve `main-windows-amd64.exe` em qualquer máquina.\n- **Nenhum compilador C é necessário.** O runtime é todo Go, então um build cruzado roda com `CGO_ENABLED=0`, e um build para esta máquina não recebe ambiente nenhum: byte a byte o mesmo build que sem `--target`.\n- **Só `build` e `export` recebem a flag**, os dois comandos que escrevem algo para uma plataforma. `check`, `emit`, `test` e `container` a recusam em vez de ignorá-la em silêncio. No `hive run`, tudo depois do ponto de entrada pertence ao programa, então um `--target` escrito ali é repassado direto para ele.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "One machine, three platforms", captionPt: "Uma máquina, três plataformas", code: `$ hive build main.hive
Compiled main.hive -> main (1s)

$ hive build main.hive --target linux/arm64
Compiled main.hive -> main-linux-arm64 (2s)

$ hive build main.hive --target=windows/amd64
Compiled main.hive -> main-windows-amd64.exe (2s)`, codePt: `$ hive build main.hive
Compiled main.hive -> main (1s)

$ hive build main.hive --target linux/arm64
Compiled main.hive -> main-linux-arm64 (2s)

$ hive build main.hive --target=windows/amd64
Compiled main.hive -> main-windows-amd64.exe (2s)`),
				Example(caption: "The three ways it says no", captionPt: "As três formas de dizer não", code: "$ hive build main.hive --target linux/sparc\nhive: `--target linux/sparc` is not a platform the Go toolchain builds for. A target is a `goos/goarch` pair — `linux/arm64`, `darwin/arm64`, `windows/amd64` — and `go tool dist list` names every one it knows.\n\n$ hive build main.hive --target\nhive: `--target` was given no platform to build for. It takes a `goos/goarch` pair — `hive build <entrypoint.hive> --target linux/arm64`.\n\n$ hive check main.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `--target` is a flag of `hive build` and `hive export`. `check` builds nothing for another platform.", codePt: "$ hive build main.hive --target linux/sparc\nhive: `--target linux/sparc` is not a platform the Go toolchain builds for. A target is a `goos/goarch` pair — `linux/arm64`, `darwin/arm64`, `windows/amd64` — and `go tool dist list` names every one it knows.\n\n$ hive build main.hive --target\nhive: `--target` was given no platform to build for. It takes a `goos/goarch` pair — `hive build <entrypoint.hive> --target linux/arm64`.\n\n$ hive check main.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `--target` is a flag of `hive build` and `hive export`. `check` builds nothing for another platform."),
			],
		),
		Slide(
			title: "Packaging an app",
			titlePt: "Empacotando um app",
			download: false,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "`hive build` writes a program; `hive export` writes an app. `hive export taps.hive --target android/arm64` produces `taps-android-arm64.apk`, ready to install.\n\nThe window needs no changes. A `hive.ui.window` already serves its own page over loopback with every asset embedded, so on a handset the platform's own WebView shows it. The program is never told it is on a phone: it serves the same tree it would serve a browser, and the same `view` answers both.\n\n- **`android/arm64` is the only Android target.** It is the only one Go links without a C cross-compiler, so the other three are refused by name, and it is what handsets run.\n- **A program with no window is refused**, not packaged: an app that shows nothing has no way to be started. `hive build` is for a program that serves or prints.\n- **No tool but Go is involved.** The file holds the program, a fixed WebView host, a binary manifest, and `assets/icon.png` as the launcher icon where the program ships one. The build writes the manifest, the resource table, the archive and its signature itself: no Android SDK, and no network.\n- **The application id comes from the entrypoint's name**: `taps.hive` is `hive.taps`. Two apps whose entrypoints are both `main.hive` are one app to the phone, and the second installs over the first, so name the entrypoint after the app.\n\nThe APK is signed with APK Signature Scheme v2, under a key made on first use at `~/.hive/android.key`. Each machine keeps one stable key, so a later build installs over an earlier one instead of being refused as a different app.",
			explanationPt: "`hive build` escreve um programa; `hive export` escreve um app. `hive export taps.hive --target android/arm64` produz `taps-android-arm64.apk`, pronto para instalar.\n\nA janela não precisa de mudança nenhuma. Uma `hive.ui.window` já serve a própria página em loopback com todos os recursos embutidos, então, num aparelho, quem a mostra é a WebView que a plataforma já tem. O programa nunca fica sabendo que está num telefone: ele serve a mesma árvore que serviria a um navegador, e a mesma `view` responde aos dois.\n\n- **`android/arm64` é o único target do Android.** É o único que o Go linka sem um compilador C cruzado, então os outros três são recusados pelo nome, e é o que os aparelhos usam.\n- **Um programa sem janela é recusado**, não empacotado: um app que não mostra nada não tem como ser iniciado. `hive build` é para um programa que serve ou imprime.\n- **Nenhuma ferramenta além do Go entra no processo.** O arquivo carrega o programa, um hospedeiro WebView fixo, um manifesto binário e `assets/icon.png` como ícone do lançador, quando o programa traz um. O próprio build escreve o manifesto, a tabela de recursos, o arquivo compactado e a assinatura: sem SDK do Android e sem rede.\n- **O id da aplicação vem do nome do ponto de entrada**: `taps.hive` vira `hive.taps`. Dois apps cujos pontos de entrada são ambos `main.hive` são um app só para o telefone, e o segundo instala por cima do primeiro; por isso, dê ao ponto de entrada o nome do app.\n\nO APK é assinado com o APK Signature Scheme v2, com uma chave criada no primeiro uso em `~/.hive/android.key`. Cada máquina mantém uma chave estável, então um build posterior instala por cima de um anterior em vez de ser recusado como um app diferente.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "A program that is also an app", captionPt: "Um programa que também é um app", code: `type Msg \{
	Tapped
}

func view(count: Int): ui.View \{
	return ui.column([ui.pad(24), ui.gap(12), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title())], "Tapped \{count} times"),
		ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Tapped()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "Tap"),
	])
}

proc update(count: mut Int, msg: Msg): Msg \{
	if msg is Msg.Tapped \{
		count += 1
	}
	return msg
}

proc main(): void \{
	spawn(ui.window("Taps", view, update), 0)
}`, codePt: `type Msg \{
	Tapped
}

func view(count: Int): ui.View \{
	return ui.column([ui.pad(24), ui.gap(12), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title())], "Tocado \{count} vezes"),
		ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Tapped()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "Toque"),
	])
}

proc update(count: mut Int, msg: Msg): Msg \{
	if msg is Msg.Tapped \{
		count += 1
	}
	return msg
}

proc main(): void \{
	spawn(ui.window("Toques", view, update), 0)
}`),
				Example(caption: "Run it here, install it there", captionPt: "Rode aqui, instale lá", code: `$ hive run taps.hive          # a window on this machine
$ hive export taps.hive --target android/arm64
Packaged taps.hive -> taps-android-arm64.apk (4s)

$ adb install taps-android-arm64.apk
Success`, codePt: `$ hive run taps.hive          # uma janela nesta máquina
$ hive export taps.hive --target android/arm64
Packaged taps.hive -> taps-android-arm64.apk (4s)

$ adb install taps-android-arm64.apk
Success`),
				Example(caption: "The four ways it says no", captionPt: "As quatro formas de dizer não", code: "$ hive export taps.hive\nhive: `hive export` needs a target, and the one it knows is `--target android/arm64`.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target android/amd64\nhive: `--target android/amd64` needs a C cross-compiler, which is the one thing a machine with the Go toolchain on it cannot be assumed to have. `android/arm64` is the only Android target Go links on its own, and it is what a handset is.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `hive export` knows one format, and it is Android's. It takes `--target android/arm64`; `linux/arm64` has no app to be packaged into.\n\n$ hive export server.hive --target android/arm64\nserver.hive:1: `server.hive` opens no window, so there is nothing for an app to show and no way to start it. `hive build` is what compiles a program that serves or prints.", codePt: "$ hive export taps.hive\nhive: `hive export` needs a target, and the one it knows is `--target android/arm64`.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target android/amd64\nhive: `--target android/amd64` needs a C cross-compiler, which is the one thing a machine with the Go toolchain on it cannot be assumed to have. `android/arm64` is the only Android target Go links on its own, and it is what a handset is.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `hive export` knows one format, and it is Android's. It takes `--target android/arm64`; `linux/arm64` has no app to be packaged into.\n\n$ hive export server.hive --target android/arm64\nserver.hive:1: `server.hive` opens no window, so there is nothing for an app to show and no way to start it. `hive build` is what compiles a program that serves or prints."),
			],
		),
		Slide(
			title: "Containers",
			titlePt: "Contêineres",
			download: false,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "`hive container main.hive` writes a Dockerfile for the program into the folder the command was run in, which is also the build context. It checks the program first, as `hive check` would, so a program that does not compile gets its errors now rather than from an image build that fails ten minutes later.\n\nBuilding the image needs nothing installed but Docker:\n\n- **The first stage downloads Go, and the compiler that wrote the file**, for the platform being built for: amd64 on an ordinary machine and arm64 on Apple silicon, so the build is native either way. The compiler is pinned in `ARG HIVEC_VERSION`, so an image never moves to a newer release on its own, and `docker build --build-arg HIVEC_VERSION=v9.9.9 .` builds with another one.\n- **The second stage is the executable alone**, on `distroless/static`: no Go, no compiler, not even a shell, only the time zones and the certificates an HTTPS call needs.\n\nWhat is not template is read from the program. A `hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...)` becomes an `EXPOSE 8080`, with a comment saying where the number came from, and a program that serves nothing exposes nothing. An import that names a repository puts `git` in the build stage, and a program that opens a database runs `go mod tidy` before anything compiles. Everything beside the Dockerfile goes into the build, so a `.dockerignore` is how to narrow it.\n\nThe result is an ordinary Dockerfile, meant to be edited. A `Dockerfile` already in the folder is never overwritten: the new one is named `Dockerfile-hive-container` instead, the command says which it wrote, and building from it takes `docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container .`. This tour is served from an image built this way.",
			explanationPt: "`hive container main.hive` escreve um Dockerfile para o programa na pasta em que o comando foi rodado, que também é o contexto do build. Ele verifica o programa antes, como o `hive check` faria, então um programa que não compila recebe os erros na hora, e não de um build de imagem que falha dez minutos depois.\n\nPara construir a imagem, só o Docker precisa estar instalado:\n\n- **O primeiro estágio baixa o Go e o compilador que escreveu o arquivo**, para a plataforma de destino: amd64 numa máquina comum e arm64 num Apple silicon, então o build é nativo nos dois casos. O compilador fica fixado em `ARG HIVEC_VERSION`, então uma imagem nunca passa sozinha para um lançamento mais novo, e `docker build --build-arg HIVEC_VERSION=v9.9.9 .` constrói com outro.\n- **O segundo estágio é só o executável**, sobre `distroless/static`: sem Go, sem compilador, nem mesmo um shell, só os fusos horários e os certificados de que uma chamada HTTPS precisa.\n\nO que não é template é lido do programa. Um `hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...)` vira um `EXPOSE 8080`, com um comentário dizendo de onde o número veio, e um programa que não serve nada não expõe nada. Um import que nomeia um repositório coloca `git` no estágio de build, e um programa que abre um banco de dados roda `go mod tidy` antes de compilar qualquer coisa. Tudo o que está ao lado do Dockerfile entra no build, então é com um `.dockerignore` que se restringe isso.\n\nO resultado é um Dockerfile comum, feito para ser editado. Um `Dockerfile` que já esteja na pasta nunca é sobrescrito: o novo se chama `Dockerfile-hive-container`, o comando diz qual escreveu, e para construir a partir dele use `docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container .`. Este tour é servido a partir de uma imagem construída assim.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "Writing it, and running what it wrote", captionPt: "Escrevendo, e rodando o que foi escrito", code: `$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -t main .
$ docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

# A Dockerfile already there is left alone, and ours is named for itself.
$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile-hive-container for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container -t main .`, codePt: `$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -t main .
$ docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

# Um Dockerfile que já esteja lá é deixado em paz, e o nosso leva nome próprio.
$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile-hive-container for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container -t main .`),
				Example(caption: "What it writes", captionPt: "O que ele escreve", code: `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
#
# Builds and runs main.hive, from nothing but this folder.
#
#   docker build -t main .
#   docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

##############################################################################
# Stage 1 — download Go and the compiler, then build
##############################################################################
FROM debian:bookworm-slim AS builder

# What Docker is building for: amd64 on an ordinary machine, arm64 on an
# Apple-silicon one or a Graviton.
ARG TARGETARCH

# ... curl, then Go itself for that architecture ...

# The compiler itself — this one, rather than whatever release is newest on
# the day the image is built.
ARG HIVEC_VERSION=v0.2.10
RUN curl -fsSL \
      "https://github.com/R0DR160HM/hive-lang/releases/download/$\{HIVEC_VERSION}/hivec-linux-$\{TARGETARCH}" \
      -o /usr/local/bin/hivec \
 && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hivec

# A static binary, so the runtime stage below needs no libc.
ENV CGO_ENABLED=0

WORKDIR /app
COPY . .

RUN hivec build /app/main.hive \
 && test -x /app/main

##############################################################################
# Stage 2 — runtime
##############################################################################
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12 AS runtime
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app/main /usr/local/bin/main

# 8080 is where this program serves: hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...) says so.
EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/main"]`, codePt: `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
#
# Builds and runs main.hive, from nothing but this folder.
#
#   docker build -t main .
#   docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

##############################################################################
# Stage 1 — download Go and the compiler, then build
##############################################################################
FROM debian:bookworm-slim AS builder

# What Docker is building for: amd64 on an ordinary machine, arm64 on an
# Apple-silicon one or a Graviton.
ARG TARGETARCH

# ... curl, then Go itself for that architecture ...

# The compiler itself — this one, rather than whatever release is newest on
# the day the image is built.
ARG HIVEC_VERSION=v0.2.10
RUN curl -fsSL \
      "https://github.com/R0DR160HM/hive-lang/releases/download/$\{HIVEC_VERSION}/hivec-linux-$\{TARGETARCH}" \
      -o /usr/local/bin/hivec \
 && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hivec

# A static binary, so the runtime stage below needs no libc.
ENV CGO_ENABLED=0

WORKDIR /app
COPY . .

RUN hivec build /app/main.hive \
 && test -x /app/main

##############################################################################
# Stage 2 — runtime
##############################################################################
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12 AS runtime
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app/main /usr/local/bin/main

# 8080 is where this program serves: hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...) says so.
EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/main"]`),
